<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kerman</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kerman is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In 1794 the last Zand ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lotf-ali-khan">Lotf Ali Khan</a>, was captured there by Agha Muhammad Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> after a prolonged siege. The city's inhabitants suffered terrible reprisals, and its fall marked the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kerman.jpg" alt="Kerman" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Foadkr · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D9%85%D8%AC%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B9%D9%87_%DA%AF%D9%86%D8%AC%D8%B9%D9%84%DB%8C%D8%AE%D8%A7%D9%86.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The brothers and their heirs fought on many fronts: against the Hamdanids of Mosul in the north, the Samanids of Khurasan and the Ziyarids of Gurgan in the east, and rival dynasties in <b>Kerman</b> and Oman, which Adud al-Dawla subdued in the late 960s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1794 he captured <b>Kerman</b> and the last Zand ruler, Lotf Ali Khan, who was blinded and killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Through the Gulf ports, Indian textiles, sugar, spices and other goods entered Iran, while <b>Kerman</b> wool, Persian silk, carpets, dried fruit and other agricultural products moved outward through the same networks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Over time, heavy taxation, social pressure and periods of persecution reduced them to small communities, concentrated around the desert cities of Yazd and <b>Kerman</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu Ali Muhammad ibn Ilyas, a commander of Sogdian origin in the service of the Samanid Empire, made himself master of <b>Kerman</b> around 932.</blockquote><a href="/state/ilyasid-dynasty">Ilyasid dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>He was also reckless, and his rivalry with the Muzaffarids of Yazd and <b>Kerman</b> drained his treasury.</blockquote><a href="/state/injuid-dynasty">Injuid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
